1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,847
Add an AI Agent node after your Sheets node, attach your Gemini model the same way

2
00:00:04,847 --> 00:00:07,590
as the last two builds, and open the Prompt field.

3
00:00:07,590 --> 00:00:12,142
By the third build this should take you about two minutes, and noticing that it now

4
00:00:12,142 --> 00:00:15,487
does is the quiet payoff of doing the same steps three times.

5
00:00:15,487 --> 00:00:19,985
This time the User Message is simple: hand over every row the Sheets node read, as

6
00:00:19,985 --> 00:00:20,533
one block.

7
00:00:20,533 --> 00:00:24,756
You are not selecting which rows matter, you are handing over all of them and

8
00:00:24,756 --> 00:00:27,992
letting the system message decide what to do with each one.

9
00:00:28,542 --> 00:00:31,793
Fair question, and the honest answer has a boundary on it.

10
00:00:31,793 --> 00:00:36,220
Over a pipeline of five, ten, forty rows, counting and summing sits comfortably

11
00:00:36,220 --> 00:00:40,704
inside what these models do reliably, and the trial exists to prove that against

12
00:00:40,704 --> 00:00:42,945
hand counts before anything rides on it.

13
00:00:42,945 --> 00:00:46,028
Over five hundred rows, stop: that is spreadsheet work.

14
00:00:46,028 --> 00:00:50,399
At that scale the right design is to let the sheet compute its own totals in a

15
00:00:50,399 --> 00:00:55,107
summary row and hand the agent those, so the model writes and the sheet counts, each

16
00:00:55,107 --> 00:00:57,181
doing the job it is actually good at.

17
00:00:57,181 --> 00:01:00,767
Priya's forty one accounts sit well inside the comfortable zone.

18
00:01:00,767 --> 00:01:04,803
Know which zone your own sheet is in before you copy this build onto it.

19
00:01:05,353 --> 00:01:09,760
That last sentence is new, and it matters more here than anywhere else so far.

20
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:11,569
Agent 1 was told what to ignore.

21
00:01:11,569 --> 00:01:13,603
Agent 2 was told what not to invent.

22
00:01:13,603 --> 00:01:18,011
This one is told to say "I'm not sure" out loud rather than quietly picking an

23
00:01:18,011 --> 00:01:18,407
answer.

24
00:01:18,407 --> 00:01:22,871
A report that admits uncertainty is more useful than one that hides it, because

25
00:01:22,871 --> 00:01:26,601
Priya can act on the first kind and gets blindsided by the second.

26
00:01:27,151 --> 00:01:31,757
The columns are named in the exact words of the header row, because Section 5.2 made

27
00:01:31,757 --> 00:01:36,308
those headers an interface and the briefing is where the other end of that contract

28
00:01:36,308 --> 00:01:36,637
lives.

29
00:01:36,637 --> 00:01:41,078
Fourteen days is not a magic number, it is Priya's chasing window: far enough out

30
00:01:41,078 --> 00:01:45,684
that a reminder still helps, near enough that it is worth interrupting a Monday for.

31
00:01:45,684 --> 00:01:50,236
Yours might be seven or thirty, and the point is that somebody chose it on purpose.

32
00:01:50,236 --> 00:01:54,513
"Named specifically" keeps the risk list concrete, because a warning without a

33
00:01:54,513 --> 00:01:56,541
client name is a mood, not an action.

34
00:01:56,541 --> 00:02:00,983
And "use only the numbers given" quietly blocks a failure you might not expect: a

35
00:02:00,983 --> 00:02:04,876
model helpfully rounding, or adjusting a figure that looks like a typo.

36
00:02:04,876 --> 00:02:06,357
The sheet is the authority.

37
00:02:06,357 --> 00:02:09,427
The agent repeats it or questions it, never improves it.

38
00:02:09,977 --> 00:02:14,492
Read the right hand column aloud and notice it survives being said in a corridor.

39
00:02:14,492 --> 00:02:18,952
That is the actual test of a director's report: can the reader repeat it, thirty

40
00:02:18,952 --> 00:02:23,077
seconds later, to someone more senior, without the sheet in front of them?

41
00:02:23,077 --> 00:02:25,139
Rows cannot be repeated in corridors.

42
00:02:25,139 --> 00:02:27,090
Sentences with one number each can.

43
00:02:27,090 --> 00:02:31,550
Everything this prompt does, the counting, the summing, the fourteen day window,

44
00:02:31,550 --> 00:02:36,009
exists to manufacture that repeatability, and nothing in it exists for any other

45
00:02:36,009 --> 00:02:36,399
reason.

46
00:02:36,399 --> 00:02:40,859
It is also why the report stays short on purpose: a third paragraph would be the

47
00:02:40,859 --> 00:02:42,364
place a wrong number hides.

48
00:02:42,364 --> 00:02:46,600
Three parts, three sentences each at most, every number traceable to a cell.

49
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:51,227
If Marcus wants more, the sheet is one click away, and the report's job was to make

50
00:02:51,227 --> 00:02:52,843
him know which click to want.

51
00:02:52,843 --> 00:02:57,414
When you adapt this build, keep the discipline: pick the two or three numbers your

52
00:02:57,414 --> 00:03:01,985
reader would repeat upward, and refuse the rest of the sheet a seat in the report,

53
00:03:01,985 --> 00:03:04,493
however interesting it looks on a quiet week.

54
00:03:05,043 --> 00:03:09,682
A word on where the draft should physically land, because "ready for review" has to

55
00:03:09,682 --> 00:03:12,253
mean somewhere you will actually be on Monday.

56
00:03:12,253 --> 00:03:16,947
Point the final step at your own eyes, whatever that means in your setup: a document

57
00:03:16,947 --> 00:03:21,586
the workflow appends to, a message to yourself, a file in a folder you open anyway.

58
00:03:21,586 --> 00:03:25,330
The one address it must not have yet is Marcus's, or your Marcus's.

59
00:03:25,330 --> 00:03:30,025
The report earns its way into a director's inbox at the end of a three-Monday trial,

60
00:03:30,025 --> 00:03:33,098
carried there by Priya, not posted there by a schedule.

61
00:03:33,098 --> 00:03:37,066
With the prompt written, your agent can now turn a table into a report.

62
00:03:37,066 --> 00:03:41,369
Before testing it, read your briefing aloud once, the standing ritual: if any

63
00:03:41,369 --> 00:03:46,064
sentence in it is one you could not say to a temp taking over the Monday report, the

64
00:03:46,064 --> 00:03:47,685
model will stumble on it too.

65
00:03:47,685 --> 00:03:52,267
Next lecture proves the build, against the exact numbers you would want checked by

66
00:03:52,267 --> 00:03:52,882
hand first.
